---
title: My Favorite Books pt. 1
blurb: Short writeups of three books I keep recommending, and the bits of each one that actually stuck.
date: 2026-10-04
tags: [misc]
---

People ask what I'm reading more often than you might think, and I never have a good
answer ready, so here are three I keep going back to.

## The Pragmatic Programmer

_Andy Hunt and Dave Thomas_

This one is about the job rather than any particular language. It's a pile of short
essays on the stuff nobody teaches you directly: how to keep knowledge in one place
instead of five, how to write code you can still change in a year, when to prototype
something cheap and throw it away, how to talk to the people you work with. The
examples have aged, since the book came out in 1999 and there's a fair amount of
Perl and CORBA in there, but the advice underneath has not. I reread chunks of it
every couple of years and get something different each time.

Takeaways:

- Don't live with broken windows. The first ignored warning or hacky workaround is
  permission for the next one, and then you're working in a codebase nobody respects/properly maintains.
- Explain the bug out loud to someone, or to a rubber duck if nobody's around. Saying
  it in order is usually enough to find it.

## House of Leaves

_Mark Danielewski_

A horror novel about a house that's bigger on the inside than the outside, except
you never get that story directly. You get a dead man's academic manuscript about a
documentary that might not exist, annotated by a guy who is falling apart as he
transcribes it, with his footnotes eating the page from below. The typography is
doing real work here. Text spirals, runs backwards, gets stranded in a corner of an
otherwise blank page, and the layout tightens up when the hallway does. It's the
only book I've read where the physical object is part of the horror. It is also long
and occasionally insufferable, and I love it.

Takeaways:

- How you lay something out is part of what it says. That goes for a novel and it
  goes for an interface.
- Nobody telling you the story is neutral. Worth remembering when you're reading
  documentation written by whoever built the thing.

## The Design of Everyday Things

_Don Norman_

Norman's whole argument is that when someone pushes a door that needs pulling, that's
not a dumb user, it's a bad door. He builds out a vocabulary for why: affordances for
what an object lets you do, signifiers for how it tells you, mapping for whether the
controls match the thing they control, feedback for whether you can tell your action
landed. Once you have the words, you can't stop noticing. I have opinions about
microwave panels now. This is the book that turned "users keep getting this wrong"
from a complaint into a bug report.

Takeaways:

- If people keep making the same mistake, the design is doing it to them. Fix the
  design.
- Make sure something visibly happens when a person acts. Silence reads as broken,
  and they'll click it again.

That's part one. I might do some others when I find the time.
